Every package includes an LGPL build of libmpv (https://mpv.io), licensed
under the GNU Lesser General Public License version 2.1 or later, with an
LGPL FFmpeg and the other libraries linked into it:

- Windows: the mpv-dev-lgpl build of https://github.com/zhongfly/mpv-winbuild
  (release named in libmpv-version.txt beside libmpv-2.dll).
- Linux: built by https://github.com/0x696c757a696f/lumtub-media-builds with
  mpv's gpl option off (release and sources in
  usr/share/doc/libmpv/manifest.txt; licences in usr/share/doc/libmpv/licenses).
- Android: the default build of
  https://github.com/Predidit/libmpv-android-video-build.
- macOS and iOS: Mpv.framework linked from MPVKit's LGPL libraries
  (https://github.com/mpvkit/MPVKit).

The Android APK includes CPython (https://www.python.org), from the Android
binary release python.org publishes for each Python version
(python-<version>-<host>.tar.gz for arm64-v8a and x86_64; for
armeabi-v7a, which python.org does not publish, built from the same source
tag by CPython's own Android/android.py; the version is
PYTHON_ANDROID_VERSION in .github/pins.env), under the Python Software
Foundation License Version 2. It is built with OpenSSL (Apache License 2.0),
SQLite (public domain), libffi (MIT), xz/liblzma (public domain), bzip2
(BSD-style) and zstd (BSD-3-Clause or GPL-2.0, taken under BSD-3-Clause); it
contains no GNU readline or gdbm. Its small launcher
(android/packaging/python-launcher.c) is Lumtub's. The certificates for
Python are the Chrome Root Store list in native/lumtub_core/roots.

The yt-dlp zip app in the Android APK is a stable release of yt-dlp (the
Unlicense, public domain), checked against its published SHA2-256SUMS.
QuickJS for YouTube's JavaScript challenges runs inside Lumtub's core (the
rquickjs crate with QuickJS, MIT License); no separate QuickJS program is
included.
